5. What are Merkle trees? A Merkle tree is a binary tree of hashes. Each leaf is the hash of a transaction, each parent is the hash of its two children combined, and the single hash at the top is the Merkle root, stored in the block header. Changing any transaction changes the root, so tampering is detected. A transaction can be verified with only O(log n) hashes instead of the whole block.
6. What is double spending? Spending the same digital coin twice, which is easy for digital data that can be copied. Blockchain prevents it: every transaction is verified by the network, recorded in order, and once confirmed in a block it cannot be changed. Waiting for more confirmations makes it safer.
7. Give real-life use cases of blockchain. Cryptocurrency payments, supply-chain tracking (food, medicine), healthcare records, land registry, digital identity, voting, cross-border payments and smart-contract based insurance.
